        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Peel plantain and cut on the diagonal into 3cm chunks."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Mix the spices in a bowl. Add plantain and toss to coat."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat oil in a deep pan to 180C. Fry plantain in batches of 6 to 8 pieces for 3 to 4 minutes, turning once, until deep golden brown."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Drain on paper towels. Serve immediately as a snack or alongside jollof rice and fried chicken."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Plantain burning before cooked through",
              "cause": "Oil too hot or plantain too ripe",
              "fix": "Reduce oil temperature to 165C and fry for a minute longer. Very ripe plantain browns faster."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Spices falling off during frying",
              "cause": "Plantain surface too wet",
              "fix": "Pat plantain dry before coating with spices. The drier the surface, the better the spice adheres."
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Kelewele is one of Ghana's most recognisable street foods and is eaten from Accra to Kumasi. It is sold after dark by vendors called kelewele women, who fry fresh batches to order from large iron woks. The dish is a common accompaniment to waakye (rice and beans) and is sold at bus stations, markets and outside bars. Fried plantain without the spice coating is called aloco in francophone West Africa."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
